<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ExternalCredential xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        🔴 THIS CREDENTIAL CARRIES NO AuthHeader PARAMETER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.

        The in-repo precedent, OpenAI_Credential, is also authenticationProtocol = Custom but
        declares a DefaultGroup AuthHeader ("Bearer {!$Credential.OpenAI_Credential.API_Key}"),
        because OpenAI authenticates by HTTP header. Cloudflare Turnstile's /siteverify endpoint
        does NOT: it takes the secret as a FORM FIELD IN THE POST BODY
        (secret=<secret>&response=<token>). An Authorization header here would be ignored by
        Cloudflare and the call would fail as an unauthenticated one.

        So the secret is referenced by TurnstileVerificationService as
        {!$Credential.Turnstile_Credential.Secret_Key} inside the request BODY, which requires
        <allowMergeFieldsInBody>true</allowMergeFieldsInBody> on the Cloudflare_Turnstile Named
        Credential. That combination — a Custom External Credential parameter merged into a body
        rather than a header — was exercised nowhere in this repo before this change and was
        PROVEN EMPIRICALLY against the org, not assumed. See ARCHITECTURE.md §3.5 and the
        TurnstileVerificationService class header for the probe and its result.

        🔴 THE SECRET IS NOT IN THIS FILE AND MUST NEVER BE. Secret_Key is created as an
        authentication parameter under the Turnstile_Principal Named Principal in Setup
        (Setup > Named Credentials > External Credentials > Turnstile Credential > Principals >
        Turnstile_Principal > Authentication Parameters > New: Name = Secret_Key), or via the
        Named Credentials Connect REST endpoint. Same posture as OpenAI_Credential's API_Key and
        SharePoint_Credential's client secret (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.2). A secret pasted into
        metadata, source control, or a debug log is a compromised secret and must be rotated at
        Cloudflare.

        ⚠ Secret_Key is intentionally ABSENT from the parameter list below. Salesforce stores
        authentication parameter VALUES encrypted and never serializes them into retrieved
        metadata, so declaring the name here would buy nothing and would invite a future
        contributor to fill in a value.
    -->
    <authenticationProtocol>Custom</authenticationProtocol>
    <!-- ⚠ description is capped at 255 characters and a 261-char one was REJECTED at deploy on
         2026-08-30 ("Value too long for field: Description maximum length is:255"). The full
         rationale lives in the XML comment above, which has no cap. Keep this line short. -->
    <description>Cloudflare Turnstile secret key for TurnstileVerificationService. The key lives in the Turnstile_Principal parameter Secret_Key, set in Setup post-deploy, never in metadata or source. Sent in the POST body, not an auth header. See ARCHITECTURE.md 3.5.</description>
    <externalCredentialParameters>
        <parameterGroup>Turnstile_Principal</parameterGroup>
        <parameterName>Turnstile_Principal</parameterName>
        <parameterType>NamedPrincipal</parameterType>
        <sequenceNumber>1</sequenceNumber>
    </externalCredentialParameters>
    <label>Turnstile Credential</label>
</ExternalCredential>
